{"author":{"id":"8d2e0405f08ac6c481f912544d6d5c0f58b2dc14a7e51ad4d499ee7a34567943"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795700027,"nonce":"7rH9Dgqwldr2me/TU4cNS30Iccg=","title":"rcwa internal-source (emitter) entry still infers k0 from the top ambient and assumes it is vacuum","message":"The emitter (internal-source) RCWA entry takes the wavenumber from the top ambient's coefficient, assuming that ambient is vacuum, so a dielectric top ambient reports the wavelength as lambda/n and divides every layer permittivity by n^2.\n\n**What goes wrong.** In `jno/rcwa.py`, `_build_emitter_problem` computes `super_coeff = float(np.real(cl[0][1]).mean())` (\"top ambient (vacuum) =\u003e coeff = k0^2\") and, when `wavelength=` is not given, `k0 = sqrt(super_coeff)`, then `layers = [(t, eps / k0^2)]`. If the top ambient has index n the code infers `k0' = n k0`. The solve itself is unchanged, since the equation only contains `k0^2 * eps`, but the reported wavelength is lambda/n and the layer permittivities in the spec read below 1 in the exit medium. This is the same defect as #246 for the plane-wave entry (a source on a dielectric substrate); the plane-wave entry was reworked for #246 and this older entry was left with the old rule. The emitter entry has no incident side, so the top ambient is just whichever ambient sits at max z, which makes the wrong-medium reading easier to hit.\n\n**Reproduce.** I confirmed this by reading the code, not by running it. Build an emitter problem (an internal volume source inside a finite layer) with a dielectric of index 1.56 as the top ambient and a vacuum bottom ambient and no `wavelength=`; the reported `spec.wavelength` is 1/1.56 of the true one.\n\n**Expected.** The reported wavelength and relative permittivities are the physical ones whichever ambient is dielectric. Take k0 from the vacuum side when one ambient is vacuum, or read each ambient's index from its absorbing-face coefficient (`k_n = n k0`); if neither is determined, require `wavelength=` rather than guess.\n\n**Where.** jno/rcwa.py `_build_emitter_problem` (the `super_coeff` / `k0` lines, ~1942-1948); the plane-wave entry (~2238) shares the pattern and is tracked in #246.\n\n**Workaround.** Pass `wavelength=` explicitly.\n\n**Direction.** Factor the k0 inference into one helper used by both entries (once #246 settles its rule), with a test using a dielectric top ambient that checks `spec.wavelength` and a layer permittivity.","files":null}]}